  "query_preview": "..An unusually moisture rich southerly flow persists\nover Utah and southwest Wyoming this evening ahead of a splitting\nPacific low centered over the Great Basin. The northern portion of\nthis split is nosing into western Utah and will move northeast\nacross the area through Friday morning. Earlier rain, heavy at\ntimes, was focused over central and southern Utah, where the best\njet support was located. However, the movement of the northern\nbranch of the trough is moving the best instability over northern\nUtah and southwest Wyoming, which will be the focus of\nprecipitation during the overnight hours. While there will be some\nembedded heavier rates, the precipitation will overall be more\nstratiform in nature, leading to less of a risk of flooding. With\nthe risk of rain primarily leaving central and southern Utah\ntonight, have canceled the going Flood Watch. Areal Flood Warnings\nremain in effect for areas that continue to see higher flows. \n\nMost precipitation will end across the area Friday morning, with\nsome lingering moisture and instability over northern portions of\nthe area, particularly near the Utah/Idaho border, keeping a\nthreat of showers in the forecast through the afternoon. Highs,\nwhich have been on the mild side all week, will tend to run 5-10F\nacross the area, with the greatest departure from normal over\nsouthern Utah. \n\nAs the northern branch of the splitting low exits into Montana and\nbeyond, the southern branch of the trough is expected to slowly\ntrack across the Desert Southwest over the weekend. This will keep\nthings relatively cool with continued unsettled weather. Scattered\nshowers will primarily be focused over southeast Utah on Saturday,\nwith the threat spreading into the higher terrain of northern Utah\nby Sunday with the low expected to make a northeastward jog.\n\nBy Monday, high temperatures are expected to return to near\nseasonal normals as weak ridging moves overhead, most likely bring\na dry day to the area. However, with no blocking ridge along the\nPacific coast, the storm track will remain open for much of the\nupcoming week, most likely resulting in a wetter than normal\nperiod for Utah and southwest Wyoming. There is still considerable\nensemble spread with regard to the potential timing, track, and\nstrength of any individual systems, however, so while confidence\nis high in an active pattern continuing, confidence remains low on\nthe potential impacts for the area.",
